<BaseLayout title="Why UXO matters | UXO.INFO" description="How unexploded ordnance can affect people, recovery, and environments.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Long after conflict</p><h1>Why UXO matters.</h1><p class="lead">The effects of explosive contamination can reach far beyond an individual device or incident: into daily movement, public services, livelihoods, recovery, and land.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container split"><div><h2>Lives remain organized around risk.</h2></div><ol class="numbered"><li><span>01</span><div><h3>People and communities</h3><p>Contamination can make ordinary routes, homes, schools, and work unsafe or uncertain—sometimes for years or decades.</p></div></li><li><span>02</span><div><h3>Recovery and access</h3><p>It can delay return, reconstruction, humanitarian assistance, agriculture, transport, and repair of essential infrastructure.</p></div></li><li><span>03</span><div><h3>Land and ecosystems</h3><p>Explosive hazards can restrict access to land and water, affecting ecosystems, biodiversity, livelihoods, and environmental recovery.</p></div></li></ol></div></section>
<section class="section section--deep"><div class="container split"><div><p class="eyebrow">No oversimplification</p><h2>Impact is local.</h2></div><div class="copy"><p>There is no single global experience of UXO. Conditions vary with geography, conflict history, type of contamination, local capacity, and the needs of people living with the risk.</p><p>That is why source material, local expertise, and careful language matter.</p><a class="action-link" href="/resources">Read the source material</a></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
